Z||000007||ounmongk||03-01-2000||05:49 PM||||yeah, I'd like to see the pics. I'm going to put a 12" SOMEWHERE in the car, just wanna see what my options are. I"ve looked at bass tubes, but that's not really what I"m looking for  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> (not loud enough) do you think mounting the subs in a box on the floor would be a good idea? (firing towards the ceiling) anyways, my friend has a dB meter if you really want to get a reading... but to tell you the truth, they're only like 20$ at Radio Shack. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||stimpy||03-01-2000||08:02 PM||||Regarding power wire for amp, would the slot marked "batt" on the fusebox be able to accomadate the power line that you are running? If you crimped a spade to the end and fit it in to the slot, it would save splicing the battery cables.  I'm no stereo pro, so there may be an obvious reason I'm not seing.||||reg||1
Z||000009||ounmongk||03-01-2000||08:11 PM||||stimpy, <BR>there are kits for installing amps that come with a nice 8 gauge power wires. I don't think wiring power from  the fuse box would be a good idea, the wires can't really handle it. and if you have a short in the system, I think you could melt some wires and start a fire. best thing to do is wire it up to the battery terminal... what kind of amp do you have? ||||reg||1
Z||000010||stimpy||03-01-2000||09:25 PM||||I am running a very small 100w class A Aura amp for my Bass Shakers. I have 10 guage wire with spade attached to the batt outlet on the box. This is how a shop told me I should wire it. They would no doubt do it different if they did the install, but I feel it is sufficiently strong to run the shakers without any drama. If in the future I run an amp for a sub, then I want to look at what the fusebox can carry or go through the firewall.<BR>BTW- Bass Shakers rock!!!!!  I do want to someday add the sub in the footwell, but the shakers definately rumble the kidneys with no loss of cabin space and no visibility to theives.||||reg||1
Z||000011||doughboySE||03-01-2000||09:51 PM||||my sub is in a box as it has to be in a sealed box. i had it in my VW GTI and we just ripped it apart and rebuilt the box to fit my car. i tried the footwell but having to be in a sealed box that was a no go <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>as for putting the subs on the floor <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> well in my case with a wife and 2 boys hehehehe thats a no go for me. the farther away from the boys the better it will keep <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> anyone with kids knows that <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>i have an 8 gauge cable hooked straight to the battery with an inline fuse (huge fuse) and thank god i have that fuse cause i blew it on sun by accidently touching a wire to the amp wow sparks flew <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>my box as you will see in my pics is not visible and after sunday will be even more unvisible (that a word)? i wanted it so that i could at a car show just fold my seat forward and leave it so the can see the sub if they wanted.||||reg||1
Z||000012||1FST2M6||03-01-2000||10:48 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/in-n-out.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/in-n-out.jpg</A>   10" in seal enclouser. Crammed in the footwell .31 cubic feet of air with 1.3lbs of polyfil.  And the db meters at radio shack aren't very acurate. Take your car to a reputiable IASCA participating shop and have them test it... that will give a better reading.. I borrowed a Radio Shack one from a friend that worked there.. it said my car hit 154+db With the windows rolled up!! NO WAY! There are people that spend 10s of thousands of dollars and can't achieve that.. took it to a real shop... 141.6 @ 47hz for nearly 20 seconds! Then my caps started to flake and they stoped it. Is anyone running a set of caps? have a pic of the install? mine are just bolted to the floor, looks rather cheezy! any ideas! their 1 farad monitors from Flint Machine (2 of them)||||reg||1
